%T A399344 14,20,10,15,13,22,16,21,5,16,21,19,25,11,19,17,16,26,23,27,9,22,24,
%U A399344 24,20,30,25,12,28,12,28,19,19,30,30,33,13,28,31,27,29,14
%N A399344 a(n) = A238690(A397500(n)).
%C A399344 Let s = A397500, [the ordered set of A397499(n) = A003557(A055932(n))]rad = A007947, and omega = A001221.
%C A399344 Consider the standard factorization facs(k) of a number k in A055932, i.e., Product_{i=1..omega(k)} prime(i)^m. We may generalize the exponential pattern demonstrated by facs(k) through f(k) = Product_{i=1..omega(k)} p_i^m, where p_i is the i-th smallest distinct prime factor of k.
%C A399344 Given f(k), we may employ A397500 as a general inventory of numbers k of the form rad(k) * Product_{i=1..omega(k)} p_i^m.
%C A399344 Then a(n) is a tight lower bound for A008479(k) where k of the form f(s(n)). For example, a(7) = 5 is the smallest value of A008479(k) for k of the form p_1(k) * p_2(k) * rad(k), since s(k) = 6 = prime(1) * prime(2).
%C A399344 Furthermore, the smallest number c for which A008479(c) = a(n) is such that c >= A398301(n).
%C A399344 Let j(n) be the smallest possible value of A008479 for k of the form f(s(n)). Then A000005(s(n)) <= a(n) <= j(n) <= binomial(bigomega(s(n)/rad(s(n))) + omega(s(n)), omega(s(n))).
%H A399344 Michael De Vlieger, <a href="/A399344/b399344.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A399344 Michael De Vlieger, <a href="/A399344/a399344.png">Log log scatterplot of a(n)</a>, n = 1..10^4, with a color function according to s(n) = A397500(n) showing prime s(n) in red, prime power s(n) in gold, squarefree composite s(n) in green, powerful s(n) not prime powers in purple, and s(n) neither squarefree nor powerful in blue.
%e A399344 Let w = A398314, d(n) = A000005(s(n)), b(n) = binomial(bigomega(s(n)/rad(s(n))) + omega(s(n)), omega(s(n))), g = A008479, where omega = A001221 and bigomega = A001222.
